\begin{document}
\maketitle

<<setup, include=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  echo = TRUE, eval = FALSE, purl = FALSE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE,
  comment = "#>", tidy = FALSE, size = "small"
)
options(width = 92)
library(CoTiMA2)
@

\begin{abstract}
This guide provides a reproducible diagnostic workflow for maximum-likelihood
models fitted with the Julia backend of CoTiMA2. It separates computational
problems from optimization and statistical-identification problems. It also
explains why a small improvement in the objective, a non-zero raw gradient,
or a large change in an unconstrained parameter need not have the same
interpretation. The examples are templates: replace object names and model
arguments with those used in the analysis being diagnosed.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{The three questions that should not be mixed}

A slow or non-converged fit can arise for very different reasons:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Computational:} Julia parallelism is unavailable, unsafe, or
    configured inefficiently.
  \item \textbf{Numerical:} the optimizer uses poor starting values, an
    unsuitable step size, or a difficult raw parameter scale.
  \item \textbf{Statistical:} some parameters are weakly identified, close to
    a boundary, or lie on a nearly flat likelihood ridge.
\end{enumerate}
Changing thread counts cannot repair weak identification, and increasing the
iteration limit cannot repair an incorrect parallel likelihood. Diagnose in
the order used below.

\section{Decision tree}

\begin{center}
\resizebox{0.98\linewidth}{!}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=7mm and 12mm,
  every node/.style={font=\small,align=center},
  action/.style={rectangle,rounded corners,draw=blue!55!black,fill=blue!4,
    text width=4.1cm,minimum height=9mm},
  decision/.style={diamond,aspect=2.2,draw=black!70,fill=gray!7,
    text width=3.1cm,inner sep=1.5pt},
  arrow/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]},thick}
]
\node[action] (start) {Record version, model arguments, starts, and timings};
\node[decision,below=of start] (valid) {Parallel checks safe?};
\node[action,left=of valid,xshift=-22mm] (serial) {Use the safe serial path; investigate backend consistency before tuning};
\node[decision,below=of valid] (conv) {Raw-gradient criterion met?};
\node[action,right=of conv,xshift=22mm] (done) {Proceed to uncertainty and transformed summaries};
\node[decision,below=of conv] (progress) {Objective and gradient still improve materially?};
\node[action,left=of progress,xshift=-22mm] (refine) {Continue once from saved estimates with a smaller step};
\node[decision,below=of progress] (stable) {Substantive transformed estimates stable?};
\node[action,right=of stable,xshift=22mm] (report) {Document practical stability and remaining raw-scale warning};
\node[action,left=of stable,xshift=-22mm] (identify) {Inspect study information, local parameters, Hessian, and model restrictions};
\draw[arrow] (start)--(valid);
\draw[arrow] (valid)--node[above]{no}(serial);
\draw[arrow] (valid)--node[right]{yes}(conv);
\draw[arrow] (conv)--node[above]{yes}(done);
\draw[arrow] (conv)--node[right]{no}(progress);
\draw[arrow] (progress)--node[above]{yes}(refine);
\draw[arrow] (progress)--node[right]{no}(stable);
\draw[arrow] (stable)--node[above]{yes}(report);
\draw[arrow] (stable)--node[above]{no}(identify);
\end{tikzpicture}
}
\end{center}

\section{Step 1: preserve a reproducible diagnostic record}

Record the installed version and preserve every expensive fit before further
experimentation.

<<record-fit, purl=FALSE>>=
packageVersion("CoTiMA2")
saveRDS(fit_julia, "fit_julia.rds")

fit_julia$optimization
fit_julia$optimization_timing
fit_julia$likelihood_timing
fit_julia$threads
fit_julia$optimizer_controls
fit_julia$warm_start
@

The most useful convergence fields are the termination code, the maximum
absolute raw gradient, the objective, the number of iterations, and the change
from the starting point. A termination such as \code{Iterations} means that
the iteration limit was reached; it does not by itself establish that the
solution is poor. Conversely, a stable objective does not establish that all
parameters are identified.

\section{Step 2: verify the parallel backend before benchmarking}

Use \code{ctmaFitConfig()} with exactly the same model-defining arguments that
will be supplied to \code{ctmaFit()}. Binary-variable and random-intercept
arguments belong at the top level, whereas computational controls belong in
\code{backendArgs}.

<<fit-config, purl=FALSE>>=
fit_config <- ctmaFitConfig(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "julia",
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  randomIntercepts = "CINT",
  scaleTime = 1/12
)

fit_config$parallel_backend_check
fit_config$benchmarks
fit_config$optimizer_pilots
fit_config$recommended
@

Interpret the consistency check before interpreting speed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item If likelihood and gradient are both safe, parallel subject processing
    may be used for optimization.
  \item If either check fails, CoTiMA2 retains the deterministic safe path.
    Increasing \code{subject\_threads} then cannot accelerate the affected
    calculation.
  \item The choice is made for the fit and retained. It is not repeatedly
    switched during optimization.
\end{itemize}

\important{A recommended thread configuration is a performance recommendation,
not a guarantee of convergence. The benchmark measures a small number of
likelihood and gradient evaluations; the full optimizer may visit much more
difficult parameter regions.}

\section{Step 3: inspect optimizer progress}

Use the complete optimization record rather than only the last console line.

<<optimizer-record, purl=FALSE>>=
list(
  objective = -fit_julia$optimization$loglik,
  iterations = fit_julia$optimization$iterations,
  stage_iterations = fit_julia$optimization$stage_iterations,
  stage_objectives = fit_julia$optimization$stage_objectives,
  stage_gradients =
    fit_julia$optimization$stage_maximum_absolute_gradients,
  maximum_raw_gradient =
    fit_julia$optimization$maximum_absolute_gradient,
  termination = fit_julia$optimization$termination_code,
  converged = fit_julia$optimization$converged
)
@

With \code{carefulFit=TRUE}, the three stages are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item population parameters while TI effects are held fixed;
  \item population and TI-effect parameters together;
  \item a jointly rescaled refinement.
\end{enumerate}
When valid estimates are explicitly supplied through \code{inits}, CoTiMA2 can
avoid unnecessarily rebuilding early starting values. Always ensure that the
new model has exactly the same raw parameterization and parameter ordering.

\section{Step 4: compare a refinement with its starting fit}

A refinement should be judged by objective improvement, gradient reduction,
and parameter stability together.

<<compare-fits, purl=FALSE>>=
old_raw <- fit_previous$coefficients$raw
new_raw <- fit_refined$coefficients$raw
raw_names <- names(coef(fit_refined, type = "raw"))
if (is.null(raw_names)) raw_names <- paste0("raw", seq_along(new_raw))

final_gradient <- as.numeric(fit_refined$optimization$gradient)
parameter_change <- new_raw - old_raw

gradient_diagnostics <- data.frame(
  index = seq_along(new_raw),
  parameter = raw_names,
  estimate = new_raw,
  change = parameter_change,
  gradient = final_gradient,
  absolute_gradient = abs(final_gradient),
  absolute_change = abs(parameter_change)
)

head(
  gradient_diagnostics[
    order(gradient_diagnostics$absolute_gradient, decreasing = TRUE),
  ],
  25
)

head(
  gradient_diagnostics[
    order(gradient_diagnostics$absolute_change, decreasing = TRUE),
  ],
  25
)
@

Large gradients with small parameter changes indicate a stiff direction.
Large parameter changes with tiny gradients indicate a flat direction. Large
raw changes may also cancel between a population parameter and its TI effect,
or may correspond to small changes after nonlinear constraints are applied.

\section{Step 5: diagnose the study design}

\subsection{Rank and conditioning of study indicators}

<<study-design, purl=FALSE>>=
standata <- prepared_diagnostic$fit$standata
tipreds <- as.matrix(standata$tipredsdata)
colnames(tipreds) <- paste0("TI", seq_len(ncol(tipreds)))

weighted_design <- cbind(Intercept = 1, tipreds)
unique_design <- unique(weighted_design)

list(
  subject_level_rank = qr(weighted_design)$rank,
  subject_level_columns = ncol(weighted_design),
  subject_level_condition_number = kappa(weighted_design, exact = TRUE),
  study_level_rank = qr(unique_design)$rank,
  study_level_columns = ncol(unique_design),
  study_level_condition_number = kappa(unique_design, exact = TRUE),
  study_level_singular_values = svd(unique_design, nu = 0, nv = 0)$d
)
@

Full rank establishes algebraic estimability of the indicator design. It does
not guarantee that every study provides enough longitudinal information for
every dynamic, diffusion, initial-condition, or random-intercept parameter.

\subsection{Persons, rows, and measurement occasions}

<<study-information, purl=FALSE>>=
subject_rows <- tabulate(
  as.integer(standata$subject),
  nbins = nrow(tipreds)
)

study_label <- ifelse(
  rowSums(tipreds) == 0,
  "reference",
  paste0("TI", max.col(tipreds, ties.method = "first"))
)

study_information <- do.call(
  rbind,
  lapply(split(subject_rows, study_label), function(x) {
    data.frame(
      n_subjects = length(x),
      n_rows = sum(x),
      minimum_waves = min(x),
      median_waves = median(x),
      mean_waves = mean(x),
      maximum_waves = max(x)
    )
  })
)

study_information
@

The number of persons is not the only relevant quantity. A study with many
persons but only two or three occasions may weakly identify diffusion or
dynamic covariance parameters. Binary outcomes additionally require adequate
numbers in both categories.

\section{Step 6: reconstruct the parameters actually used by each study}

Under dummy coding, a raw study parameter is generally the population or
reference parameter plus the corresponding TI effect. Inspecting TI effects
alone can therefore exaggerate movement when the two components cancel.

<<study-specific-raw, purl=FALSE>>=
nbase <- as.integer(standata$nparams)
neffects <- length(raw_names) - nbase
effect_setup <- as.matrix(standata$TIPREDEFFECTsetup)

study_specific_raw <- function(raw) {
  base <- raw[seq_len(nbase)]
  effects <- raw[nbase + seq_len(neffects)]
  designs <- unique(tipreds)
  result <- matrix(
    rep(base, times = nrow(designs)),
    nrow = nrow(designs), byrow = TRUE
  )
  for (s in seq_len(nrow(designs))) {
    for (p in seq_len(nbase)) {
      for (j in seq_len(ncol(designs))) {
        effect_index <- effect_setup[p, j]
        if (effect_index > 0 && designs[s, j] != 0) {
          result[s, p] <- result[s, p] +
            effects[effect_index] * designs[s, j]
        }
      }
    }
  }
  rownames(result) <- ifelse(
    rowSums(designs) == 0,
    "reference",
    paste0("TI", max.col(designs, ties.method = "first"))
  )
  colnames(result) <- raw_names[seq_len(nbase)]
  result
}

local_previous <- study_specific_raw(old_raw)
local_current <- study_specific_raw(new_raw)
local_change <- local_current - local_previous

local_change_diagnostics <- data.frame(
  study = rep(rownames(local_change), ncol(local_change)),
  parameter = rep(colnames(local_change), each = nrow(local_change)),
  previous = as.vector(local_previous),
  current = as.vector(local_current),
  change = as.vector(local_change)
)
local_change_diagnostics$absolute_change <-
  abs(local_change_diagnostics$change)

head(
  local_change_diagnostics[
    order(local_change_diagnostics$absolute_change, decreasing = TRUE),
  ],
  30
)
@

\section{Step 7: compare constrained point estimates without a Hessian}

Raw ctsem parameters may be unconstrained optimization coordinates. Apply
ctsem's transformations without re-optimizing or calculating uncertainty by
using \code{ctmaTransformJuliaFit()}.

<<point-transform, purl=FALSE>>=
previous_transformed <- ctmaTransformJuliaFit(
  fit_previous,
  setup = prepared_diagnostic
)

refined_transformed <- ctmaTransformJuliaFit(
  fit_refined,
  setup = prepared_diagnostic
)

previous_transformed$coefficients$matrices
refined_transformed$coefficients$matrices

previous_transformed$coefficients$study
refined_transformed$coefficients$study
@

\warningbox{This function returns point transformations only. It does not
create standard errors, confidence intervals, a covariance matrix, or evidence
of convergence. Use \code{ctAddJuliaSamples()} only after reliable uncertainty
information is available.}

If the constrained matrices and scientifically relevant effects are stable
while a few raw coordinates continue to move, the remaining problem is likely
to be raw-scale curvature or weak identification. If the constrained effects
also change materially, the fit should not yet be interpreted.

\section{Step 8: use the Hessian only after point diagnostics}

The observed Hessian is useful for locating flat and boundary directions, but
it can be expensive and should not be calculated merely because optimization
stopped. Inspect its symmetric eigenvalues and condition number when the point
diagnostics remain ambiguous.

<<hessian-diagnostics, purl=FALSE>>=
hessian_symmetric <- (raw_hessian + t(raw_hessian)) / 2
eigenvalues <- eigen(hessian_symmetric, symmetric = TRUE)$values
positive <- eigenvalues[eigenvalues > 0]

list(
  minimum = min(eigenvalues),
  maximum = max(eigenvalues),
  nonpositive = sum(eigenvalues <= 0),
  near_zero = sum(abs(eigenvalues) < 1e-6),
  positive_condition_number = max(positive) / min(positive),
  smallest = head(sort(eigenvalues), 10)
)
@

A few very small negative eigenvalues can reflect numerical error near a flat
optimum. Substantial negative curvature, many near-zero eigenvalues, or an
extreme condition number require closer model inspection. Regularizing a
Hessian can make inversion possible, but does not create information that the
data do not contain.

\section{Reference studies, dummy coding, and WEC}

Changing the reference study does not change the likelihood surface in exact
arithmetic when the models are algebraically equivalent. It does change the
raw coordinates, starting values, scaling, and optimizer path. Consequently,
a different reference study can genuinely make numerical optimization easier
even though it cannot solve structural non-identification.

A useful reference study is usually reasonably large, has many informative
measurement occasions, contains both categories of every binary outcome, and
is not close to a variance or correlation boundary. A very small or atypical
reference study places the common raw intercept at a poorly determined point
and forces many large study deviations.

Weighted effect coding can reduce dependence on one arbitrary reference and
place the population parameter closer to a weighted average study. This may
improve scaling. It does not add information and cannot remove a likelihood
ridge. CoTiMA now separates the contrast basis from the parameter families:

\begin{verbatim}
studyCoding = "weighted"
studyVarying = c("CINT", "DIFFUSION", "T0VAR")
\end{verbatim}

Add \code{"DRIFT"} only when study-specific drift parameters are intended.
The older \code{WEC=TRUE} interface is deprecated and maps to the weighted,
parameter-selective specification above. This coding is constructed by
\code{ctmaFit()} and is therefore identical for Stan and Julia backends.

\section{Reproducible validation of the native ctsem Julia backend}

The package source contains a separate engineering validation suite for the
experimental \code{backend="ctsemjulia"}.  It simulates known subject- and
study-level heterogeneity and compares the estimated standard deviations with
their generating values on ctsem's unconstrained raw scale.  The full suite
tests CINT, DRIFT, DIFFUSION, and T0VAR and follows each fit with adaptive
quadrature through \code{ctLaplaceCheck()}.

Open Terminal, change to the CoTiMA2 project directory, and execute:

\begin{verbatim}
Rscript tools/ctsemjulia-validation-suite.R --mode=quick --cores=4
\end{verbatim}

The more informative full run is started with:

\begin{verbatim}
Rscript tools/ctsemjulia-validation-suite.R --mode=full --cores=4
\end{verbatim}

The same commands work under macOS and Ubuntu.  If a separate R library
contains the required ctsem development version, add
\code{--development-library=/path/to/library}.  If Julia is not discovered,
add \code{--julia-bindir=/path/to/julia/bin}.  Complete instructions are in
\code{tools/CTSEMJULIA\_VALIDATION.md}.

The run creates \code{REPORT.md}, \code{summary.csv}, and
\code{validation-results.rds} under
\code{~/CoTiMA\_Benchmark/ctsemjulia-validation/}.  A PASS is an engineering
screen, not proof of unbiasedness or correct interval coverage.  Replication
over multiple seeds and model dimensions remains necessary.

For a resumable Monte Carlo validation, use the separate controller.  Each
replication runs in an independent R/Julia process, and completed scenario
files are reused after an interruption only when their scenario, seed, and
design dimensions match:

\begin{verbatim}
Rscript tools/ctsemjulia-monte-carlo.R \
  --mode=full --replications=10 \
  --workers=4 --cores-per-fit=4
\end{verbatim}

The aggregate report includes convergence rates, bias, relative bias, RMSE,
absolute error, Laplace/quadrature gaps, execution times, and 95\% interval
coverage when ctsem exposes finite interval limits.  Use one worker on a
laptop initially.  On a many-core workstation, first benchmark a small batch
and ensure that \code{workers * cores-per-fit} leaves at least one physical
core free.  Full details, output files, and restart options are described in
\code{tools/CTSEMJULIA\_VALIDATION.md}.

Do not treat a non-hierarchical Stan or CoTiMA2-Julia fit as a numerical gold
standard for this validation.  Such a model estimates study fixed effects or
a pooled fixed effect, whereas the native Laplace model integrates Gaussian
random effects.  Their likelihoods and estimands are therefore different.  A
speed comparison is meaningful only after the competing backends express the
same hierarchical model.

\section{A concise stopping rule}

Do not continue merely because the raw gradient exceeds a conventional target.
Continue a refinement when the objective, gradient, and constrained estimates
are still improving materially. Stop and investigate identification when:
\begin{itemize}
  \item additional hours produce negligible objective improvement;
  \item large raw movements occur in directions with very small gradients;
  \item population and TI effects cancel in the study-specific parameters;
  \item constrained estimates are stable but raw coordinates drift; or
  \item the Hessian has near-zero directions or extreme conditioning.
\end{itemize}

When reporting a practically stable but formally non-converged solution,
retain the warning, document the tolerance and termination code, report the
refinement comparison, and avoid presenting Hessian-based uncertainty as
routine inferential output.

\end{document}
